Finding: {"title":"Bearer token cache is a single mutable instance field with no concurrency guard — concurrent requests can race on authenticate()","severity":"MEDIUM","area":"Concurrency / correctness","files":["src/lib/micamp.ts"],"detail":"`MiCampClient.authenticate()` (L66-105) checks `this.bearerToken` / `this.tokenExpiresAt`, and on cache-miss performs an async fetch before writing the new token. Multiple concurrent calls (e.g. several simultaneous `/micamp/payments` requests for the same school, since `loadMiCampConfig` constructs a new client per-request per L59 — so this specific race is mitigated per-request, but any code path that reuses a single client instance across concurrent calls, or a future refactor that caches the MiCampClient itself, will race) will all see `bearerToken == null` simultaneously and fire N redundant authenticate calls. Not a correctness bug today because `loadMiCampConfig` creates a fresh client per request (so no shared mutable state currently), but the class itself is not safe for the caching pattern its own field names (`bearerToken`, `tokenExpiresAt`) imply, and is a latent bug if the client is ever cached/reused (a natural future optimization given the explicit `TOKEN_CACHE_MS` constant).","impact":"Currently benign (no shared instances), but the class's own caching design will produce redundant/racy auth calls the moment someone caches a MiCampClient across requests to reduce auth overhead — a very likely future change given the code already computed a 55-minute cache window that is never actually exploited across requests.","recommendation":"Either remove the misleading token-cache fields until an actual shared-instance pattern is introduced, or if adding one, guard `authenticate()` with an in-flight promise so concurrent callers await the same authentication request.","confidence":"low"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

